Jay Maynard wrote in message ...
>I bought a VAX 4000/300 (according to the front of the box) at a NASA
>surplus auction

As a unix guy, you will probably want to find a vms guy to sell it to for
maybe $100...

If you had bought two of them together, and you found a vms guy, he could
show you something that you would find rather amazing: the ability of two
computers to share the same disks without any difficulty, and the 4000-300
shipped almost a decade ago.

While the cables to the disks will look like they are scsi-like, they are
actually DSSI which was based on top of SCSI, except more robust.  Each of
the disks can talk to up to 7 hosts concurrently and keep each conversation
straight, something that SCSI disks have learned to do in the past few
years.  And each of the host computers can talk to each other over the DSSI
bus, something that is still considered impractical for SCSI.  Fiber Channel
will begin to do (in real products) what DSSI has been doing for a long
time, but that isn't surprising since IBM designed Fibre Channel to do what
CI and DSSI have been doing for a long, long time.

Depending on what NASA was doing with them, those systems might have 32MB or
more memory which was, and still is, quite sufficient to do a lot with VMS.
The disks are probably on the order of 500 meg, which is also a lot for VMS.

The problem with getting unix running on those systems is the storage.  DSSI
is part of the Digital Storage Architecture (DSA) and DSA defines a model
that includes a port driver and a class driver.  If you follow the evolution
of Windows drivers, then you might have heard of miniport and class drivers.
Well, DSA specified that model back in the early 80s, and there were
hardware implementations of the port interface and the drives used MSCP to
talk to the host class driver.  A unix implementation would have to
implement a port driver and then two class drivers, one for disk and one for
tape.  Neither of these hardware interfaces has been made public.  It would
actually be a lot easier to "reverse engineer" than SCSI since all it would
take would be a few points to some papers and books on VMScluster related
topics and a couple of data structure definitions and you would be able to
pretty much figure it out if you are a logical thinker.

But you would have a major problem getting the system boot strapped unless
you found a Qbus SCSI adapter and driver along with another VAX system that
supported some unix.

I'm trying to figure out what someone who wasn't interested in vms would
find of value in that system.  The boards in it are Qbus, and you would have
to go back a ways for find a Qbus system that unix supported; it would be a
VAX 3000 system in the same style cab; the VAX 3100s which look like big
desktop computers don't have Qbus.  The memory and CPU board are specific to
that box and the chips themself aren't worth much.  The disks and tape are
DSSI and not SCSI.  Besides the tape is way distant from the modern DLT
drives (TK70 I think).

The power supply is probably pretty good.  And the box is quite sturdy so
you could use it to hold up a 50 inch TV without too much concern.

Find a VMS kind of guy and point him to the DECUS VAX/VMS hobbiest license
info.

mulp





