Gregg E Economou <geest3+@pitt.edu> wrote:
>If I can get my hands on a VAX 4000, I'm willing to contribute to a port.
>(on a mostly unrelated note, I'm currently working on a straight port of
>4.3-Reno to the PMAXen -- the goal being something which does not have the

You are aware that 4.4BSD supported the pmax?  If you're actually licensed
for 32V UNIX (which anyone can be for $100 now, and which you'll need to
be in order to get *either* 4.3-Reno or 4.4BSD) you might just try that.

AFAIK NetBSD's performance on the pmax, particularly recently, is
significantly better than 4.4BSD's was.  That's just anecdotal, though;
I've run Ultrix, Sprite, and NetBSD on my pmax, and certainly NetBSD is
both the most comprehensible at the source level (of NetBSD and Sprite;
I don't have Ultrix sources) and the best performer.

FYI, the new VM system (Chuck Cranor's "UVM") has significantly improved
NetBSD's performance on all supported architectures; the pmax either has
UVM now or is about to -- I haven't looked in the past couple of weeks.

I'm sure Jonathan can speak to this at quite some length.

>complexities and layered approach of NetBSD -- and I'd plan to port it to
>the VAX 4000 as well, if i can find one cheap or free) (however, i do plan
>to snarf a lot of code from NetBSD for some device drivers and such ;)

In my experience, where NetBSD appears "more layered" than 4.3-Reno or 4.4,
it's either the consequence of the hasty pasting in of the Mach VM system
before the 4.4 release -- but we have a new VM system now, which is cleaner --
or because the CSRG code hid a poor abstraction beneath a sea of #ifdefs,
or the like.  Certainly we've reorganized the code significantly, but in
my experience it's far, far easier to understand what's going on in today's
NetBSD kernel than in the Net2 (Reno-era) code that it began with.

>From what I undrstand, the only places where work woudl need to be done
>would be in supporting the slightly different console systems for each
>model of VAX4k, and the different processors (there is quite a difference
>from the 200/300 to the later model boxen) and then the real biggie- DSSI 
>support. From what ive heard DSSI is like SCSI but then not like it, and there
>arent many specs on it publicly available.. Anybody at DEC care to drop any
>old documentation in our direction? 

Other vendors did build DSSI hardware, and that might be one avenue.  If DEC
could be persuaded to donate relevant portions of the Ultrix source (and they
did that for 4.3 in the past, so it's probably not inconcievable) that might
be another.  I do know that DSSI was discussed as "another SCSI-like protocol"
when the SCSI/ATAPI issue was addressed before 1.3.

